# 02 - The Emergency That Never LeftIMG_4813.png
by Obinna D. Ugoeke, Snr

Some emergencies do not end when the siren stops.

They end in public, but they continue in private. People clap and say, "Thank God it is over." They go back to their normal lives. They do not know that for you, it never ended.

That is what happened to him.

The day it happened, everybody was there. Calls. Messages. Prayers. Food. People said, "If you need anything, call me."

And he believed them.

Then a week passed. Then a month. Then the messages stopped. The calls stopped. Life moved on — for everybody except him.

He still heard the sound at night. He still woke up at 3am checking if it was happening again. He still flinched when his phone rang. But there was nobody to tell, because the emergency was officially over.

So he learned to carry it quietly.

He went to work. He laughed. He posted. He said, "I am fine." People believed him because it was easier to believe than to ask twice.

But inside, he was still in that day. Still standing in that room. Still waiting for someone to come back and say, "We have not forgotten you."

Nobody came.

That is the cruelest part of surviving something. People celebrate that you survived, then they leave you alone to survive the surviving.

If you know someone like that, do not just ask once. Ask again. A month later. Six months later. A year later.

Because some people are still bleeding from a wound everybody thinks has healed.

And sometimes, the second ask is the one that saves them.

